From c70e938e26a4ebad447913fe32b71baf6f8cb7f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 01:27:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] i686: precise inline-asm scratch-demand scan replaces
 blanket callee-saved clobber
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The i686 prologue marked ALL callee-saved registers clobbered whenever
any inline-asm operand used a generic register class (r/q/g/rm). In
arch/x86/boot every function reads segment registers via one-operand
"=rm" asm, so every such function paid 3-4 push/pop pairs and lost
its entire register file for values spanning the asm — the single
largest architectural size penalty on the 32K setup binary.

New collect_inline_asm_callee_saved_i686 proves per asm block that the
scratch allocator's worst-case demand fits in the caller-saved prefix
(%ecx/%edx) of its fixed pool order [ecx,edx,esi,edi,eax,ebx]:
demand counts every non-tied/non-immediate/non-specific operand
(x2 for 64-bit pairs, memory staging included), availability drops
for specific c/d constraints and ecx/edx clobbers. Only when some
block's demand exceeds the prefix does the conservative blanket
marking return.

boot/regs.o: 261 -> 186 bytes (GCC: 55); whole realmode C corpus
42175 -> 40737. Validation: 780/780 unit, 50/50 correctness,
regparm3_abi_conformance green at all opt levels.
